WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.500
This is Chapter 5: Lifecycle
Workflows custom extensions.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.052
A custom task extension hands a leaver,
a joiner or a mover to code you wrote,

5
00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:18.037
and then waits for that
code to say what happened.

6
00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:20.467
The chapter makes sure it always says,

7
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:23.288
says the truth, and says
it to the right cloud.

8
00:00:23.288 --> 00:00:27.328
We'll start from the Logic App
definition Microsoft publishes,

9
00:00:27.328 --> 00:00:30.668
see what it leaves to you,
walk the program's Logic App,

10
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:33.776
registry and tools, then
two labs and the dangers.

11
00:00:33.776 --> 00:00:39.280
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.542
Why does this chapter matter?

13
00:00:44.542 --> 00:00:48.375
Book 03 splits the work:
provisioning creates accounts,

14
00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:52.542
and Lifecycle Workflows act on
accounts that already exist.

15
00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:57.611
The built-in tasks handle the account
itself, but everything else in a leaver,

16
00:00:57.611 --> 00:01:00.507
the PIV credential, the
devices, the servers,

17
00:01:00.507 --> 00:01:04.530
goes through a custom task
extension, which is code you write.

18
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:09.884
Book 03 also sets three rules: one
reusable Logic App per integration,

19
00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:14.580
block the account first, and make
custom tasks safe to repeat.

20
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.965
Learn sets the contract on the other
side: how the Logic App is started,

21
00:01:18.965 --> 00:01:22.580
how long the workflow waits,
and how the answer comes back.

22
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.460
Let's see where that runs.

23
00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.623
Now, which cloud.

24
00:01:29.623 --> 00:01:34.588
The callback goes to Microsoft Graph,
and the host depends on the tenant:

25
00:01:34.588 --> 00:01:39.806
a GCC tenant like this estate's keeps
the worldwide endpoint, while GCC High

26
00:01:39.806 --> 00:01:44.772
and DoD each have their own, and a token
for one cloud won't work in another.

27
00:01:44.772 --> 00:01:46.761
The Logic App's side matters too.

28
00:01:46.761 --> 00:01:51.198
Learn documents the proof-of-possession
policy only for commercial Azure.

29
00:01:51.198 --> 00:01:56.010
For Azure Government it gives nothing, so
the builder refuses rather than guess.

30
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.952
Licensing and availability are
covered in all the US clouds,

31
00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:02.948
and the tenant limits
are the same everywhere.

32
00:02:02.948 --> 00:02:05.520
Next, Microsoft's own sample.

33
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.982
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

34
00:02:10.982 --> 00:02:15.019
The admin center can create an
extension and a new Logic App together.

35
00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:19.952
For an existing Logic App, Learn publishes
one workflow definition to paste in:

36
00:02:19.952 --> 00:02:23.594
a trigger that describes what
Lifecycle Workflows sends,

37
00:02:23.594 --> 00:02:28.070
one callback action, and a gap in the
middle labelled "add your logic here".

38
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.783
That's Microsoft's only sample; a
search found no template anywhere else.

39
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:38.213
Because it lives only inside a web page,
the repository pins the page with checked

40
00:02:38.213 --> 00:02:43.319
hashes, and a tiny interpreter runs the
definition, unchanged, against fakes.

41
00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:45.380
So you see what it really does.

42
00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.438
Running Learn's definition
against the fakes

43
00:02:51.438 --> 00:02:53.867
shows what it gets right
and what it leaves to you.

44
00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:56.357
It gets the identity right: no secret,

45
00:02:56.357 --> 00:03:00.755
just the Logic App's managed
identity, and the program keeps that.

46
00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:06.275
But the callback is hard-wired to the
worldwide cloud and the beta endpoint,

47
00:03:06.275 --> 00:03:09.480
so in GCC High or DoD it
simply never arrives.

48
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.701
It trusts whatever path it's handed,

49
00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:16.142
which means a forged payload could
aim that identity's token anywhere.

50
00:03:16.142 --> 00:03:20.367
And it always says the work completed,
even when the revocation failed.

51
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.580
Each of those has a fix, pinned by a test.

52
00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.518
The second half of the gap table is about
what happens once you add real work.

53
00:03:31.518 --> 00:03:34.798
If your added step fails,
Learn's definition sends nothing,

54
00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:39.105
so the workflow sits there for the whole
wait before it calls the task failed.

55
00:03:39.105 --> 00:03:41.868
There's no protection
against doing the work twice.

56
00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:44.558
The subject's name, email and manager land

57
00:03:44.558 --> 00:03:47.168
in run history for anyone who can read it.

58
00:03:47.168 --> 00:03:51.535
The retries are implicit, and the
authorization policy is typed by hand,

59
00:03:51.535 --> 00:03:54.792
with a placeholder value
that would block every call.

60
00:03:54.792 --> 00:03:58.080
The program's definition closes each one.

61
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.130
One row needs a word.

62
00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:07.294
The page prints a placeholder for
the value that identifies Lifecycle

63
00:04:07.294 --> 00:04:10.893
Workflows as the caller, and
copying it would block every call.

64
00:04:10.893 --> 00:04:15.039
The chapter traces the real value:
the page's own bearer-token row,

65
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:18.559
the page's history, Microsoft's
token claims reference,

66
00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:23.643
and the docs repository's list of known
values all point to the same identifier.

67
00:04:23.643 --> 00:04:28.268
The code uses it, a test pins it, and
Lab 2 still checks it in the tenant.

68
00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:31.505
That leads to the verdict: keep
Learn's shape and trigger,

69
00:04:31.505 --> 00:04:35.660
but don't run it as published for
a leaver's credential revocation.

70
00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:40.810
Now the program's code.

71
00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:45.499
There's one Logic App definition, and
every integration deploys the same one;

72
00:04:45.499 --> 00:04:49.518
what changes is data, four parameters:
the Graph host for the cloud,

73
00:04:49.518 --> 00:04:53.835
the downstream system and its audience,
and which workflows it serves.

74
00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:55.773
Each run goes in four steps.

75
00:04:55.773 --> 00:04:59.572
First it checks the request, and
if the callback path isn't the

76
00:04:59.572 --> 00:05:03.523
resume path for this very task, it
stops and sends nothing at all.

77
00:05:03.523 --> 00:05:08.201
Then it looks up the operation, calls the
downstream system with bounded retries,

78
00:05:08.201 --> 00:05:12.830
and always calls back, telling the
truth about whether the work succeeded.

79
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.743
Two ideas hold the design together.

80
00:05:18.743 --> 00:05:20.642
The first is idempotency.

81
00:05:20.642 --> 00:05:22.663
The key isn't a Microsoft feature;

82
00:05:22.663 --> 00:05:26.855
it's a promise the downstream system
makes to do the work once per key.

83
00:05:26.855 --> 00:05:32.382
That matters because the same task can
arrive twice: the extension may retry its

84
00:05:32.382 --> 00:05:34.308
call, a workflow can be rerun,

85
00:05:34.308 --> 00:05:37.992
and Book 03 resumes a partial
leaver where it stopped.

86
00:05:37.992 --> 00:05:42.213
The second is the registry: one
reviewed entry per integration,

87
00:05:42.213 --> 00:05:45.054
holding every value the deployment needs.

88
00:05:45.054 --> 00:05:47.917
Its extension settings
have sensible defaults,

89
00:05:47.917 --> 00:05:51.630
and the builder refuses anything
outside Learn's ranges.

90
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.217
The tooling is one script
with five commands.

91
00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:02.423
Lint applies twelve rules to any
definition, including Microsoft's,

92
00:06:02.423 --> 00:06:05.801
and one of them adds up every
possible retry wait so the

93
00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:09.105
callback can't arrive after
the workflow has given up.

94
00:06:09.105 --> 00:06:11.614
Run plays a definition against the fakes,

95
00:06:11.614 --> 00:06:14.493
with failures and repeat
deliveries on demand.

96
00:06:14.493 --> 00:06:17.730
Build refuses to write anything
while there's a finding.

97
00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.319
And the two checks compare what's
really in the tenant with the registry,

98
00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:24.419
including Book 03's leaver order:

99
00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:28.930
block the account and revoke its tokens
first, then run the custom task.

100
00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:32.520
A failed revocation shouldn't
stop the cleanup after it.

101
00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.573
Authorization is where hand-typed
settings usually drift,

102
00:06:39.573 --> 00:06:41.932
so the builder writes
it from the registry.

103
00:06:41.932 --> 00:06:45.707
On the Logic App, shared access
signatures are switched off and

104
00:06:45.707 --> 00:06:49.771
Learn's proof-of-possession policy
goes in with Learn's exact claims.

105
00:06:49.771 --> 00:06:54.028
On the extension, the code asks for
proof-of-possession, following Learn's

106
00:06:54.028 --> 00:06:58.570
security guidance even though one example
still shows the older bearer type.

107
00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.713
And the identity stays small: because
the Logic App is an authorized

108
00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:06.857
app on the extension, it can call
back with no Graph permission at all.

109
00:07:06.857 --> 00:07:10.319
The tenant-wide alternative is
what Learn advises against.

110
00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:12.040
Now, who does this work?

111
00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:19.389
Learn says several things
differently on different pages,

112
00:07:19.389 --> 00:07:24.090
and the chapter writes each one down so
nobody quietly loosens the code later.

113
00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:28.465
The wait range differs between pages,
so the code takes the narrower one.

114
00:07:28.465 --> 00:07:32.365
The workflow limit differs, so the
chapter plans for the lower one.

115
00:07:32.365 --> 00:07:36.878
Even the identifier for the custom
task differs, within a single page.

116
00:07:36.878 --> 00:07:41.020
In each case the code picks the
safer or better-supported reading,

117
00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.240
and where it matters, Lab 2 reads
back what the tenant actually does.

118
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.778
That's the end of the code.

119
00:07:46.778 --> 00:07:48.540
Now, who's involved.

120
00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.352
Who is this for?

121
00:07:53.352 --> 00:07:58.260
Developers and identity engineers who
build the integrations behind joiners,

122
00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.828
movers and leavers, and the
reviewers who approve each one.

123
00:08:01.828 --> 00:08:06.553
You'll want to read JSON and Python,
and you don't need a tenant until Lab 2.

124
00:08:06.553 --> 00:08:08.127
The roles are worth noticing.

125
00:08:08.127 --> 00:08:13.202
Creating an extension takes the Lifecycle
Workflows administrator role plus a

126
00:08:13.202 --> 00:08:18.277
contributor role on the Logic App's resource
group; the operator role isn't enough.

127
00:08:18.277 --> 00:08:21.835
The Logic App itself needs no
Graph permission to call back,

128
00:08:21.835 --> 00:08:26.190
and its downstream access should
be as narrow as the target allows.

129
00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.602
Lab 1 needs only Python on a
laptop: no tenant, no network.

130
00:08:34.602 --> 00:08:36.741
You start with Microsoft's definition.

131
00:08:36.741 --> 00:08:41.311
The linter finds eight problems, and
when you run it for a GCC High tenant,

132
00:08:41.311 --> 00:08:46.502
the callback heads to the worldwide cloud
and the task fails at the end of the wait.

133
00:08:46.502 --> 00:08:49.534
Switch the cloud to
worldwide and it completes,

134
00:08:49.534 --> 00:08:52.565
which is exactly why the
problem is easy to miss.

135
00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:57.667
Then the program's definition: a clean
lint, and a GCC High run that survives

136
00:08:57.667 --> 00:09:03.210
two transient failures, does the work
once, and calls back to the right cloud.

137
00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.348
The second half of Lab 1 is
about watching the guards work.

138
00:09:10.348 --> 00:09:14.510
Shorten the wait and the linter
warns that retries could outlast it.

139
00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:16.739
Make the downstream fail every time,

140
00:09:16.739 --> 00:09:21.047
and the task is reported failed
straight away instead of an hour later.

141
00:09:21.047 --> 00:09:24.997
Deliver the same task twice,
and the work still happens once.

142
00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:26.755
Then build the deployment files,

143
00:09:26.755 --> 00:09:30.335
and watch the builder refuse when
you point it at Azure Government.

144
00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:34.435
Break the leaver order in a fixture
and the workflow check catches it.

145
00:09:34.435 --> 00:09:36.935
Finally, fifty-five tests pass.

146
00:09:36.935 --> 00:09:40.070
Remember, though: fakes aren't a tenant.

147
00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.070
Lab 2 moves to a test
tenant, never production.

148
00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:51.515
You let the admin center create the extension
and Logic App the way Learn describes,

149
00:09:51.515 --> 00:09:55.187
then record exactly what it made
and compare it with what the builder

150
00:09:55.187 --> 00:09:58.795
would have written; any difference
is a finding against the builder.

151
00:09:58.795 --> 00:10:01.248
Then you swap in the program's definition,

152
00:10:01.248 --> 00:10:06.082
build a small leaver workflow in the right
order, and run it for one fictional user.

153
00:10:06.082 --> 00:10:09.182
That run also confirms
the real callback path.

154
00:10:09.182 --> 00:10:14.146
Finally, drill the failure: the task
should fail within minutes, not an hour.

155
00:10:14.146 --> 00:10:15.990
Then back everything out.

156
00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.404
Each of these dangers ends the same way:

157
00:10:22.404 --> 00:10:26.815
a leaver whose access isn't really
gone, or a record that says it is.

158
00:10:26.815 --> 00:10:30.842
The quietest are the callback
problems: no callback on failure,

159
00:10:30.842 --> 00:10:34.715
a callback that claims success,
or one sent to the wrong cloud.

160
00:10:34.715 --> 00:10:37.562
Others are about trust: a forged payload,

161
00:10:37.562 --> 00:10:41.790
a broad permission, or a
bearer-token extension left behind.

162
00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.488
And some are about order:
doing the work twice,

163
00:10:44.488 --> 00:10:47.915
or running the custom task
before the account is blocked.

164
00:10:47.915 --> 00:10:53.590
Notice the pattern again: each guard is a
lint rule or a check that runs every time.

165
00:10:57.340 --> 00:10:58.690
Here's what done looks like.

166
00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:03.532
Each integration has one reviewed registry
entry, one Logic App and one identity,

167
00:11:03.532 --> 00:11:07.290
and the deployed pieces came from
the builder with a clean lint.

168
00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:11.027
The extension uses proof-of-possession
and waits for an answer;

169
00:11:11.027 --> 00:11:15.913
shared access signatures are off; and the
callback host matches the tenant's cloud,

170
00:11:15.913 --> 00:11:19.003
which for this estate means
the worldwide endpoint.

171
00:11:19.003 --> 00:11:23.256
Then the proofs: a repeat delivery
did the work once, a forced failure

172
00:11:23.256 --> 00:11:27.814
reported back within minutes, and
every leaver blocks the account first.

173
00:11:27.814 --> 00:11:31.140
Next, Chapter 6 moves
Group Policy as code.

174
00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.310
Thanks for watching.
